\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Tick interval\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The current concept uses per-second updates.\uc0\u8232 That is acceptable conceptually, but implementation should be careful.\
Internally, it may be better to compute quality based on elapsed time since last update rather than literally firing one update every second forever. That avoids performance issues and makes offline or inactive-tab progression easier later.\
Design-wise, however, the rule should be expressed as:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls28\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 quality increases each second of active beneficial conditions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Implementation can then choose whether to simulate this as:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls29\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 real per-second ticking\
\ls29\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 accumulated elapsed-time calculations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The design intent is what matters.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

